03513336181640625, -0.037353515625, -0.0003802776336669922, 0.0260162353515625, -0.005031585693359375, -0.004978179931640625, -0.0020465850830078125, 0.014892578125, 0.01837158203125, 0.0171051025390625, -0.051025390625, -0.01110076904296875, -0.03997802734375, 0.006916046142578125, 0.005443572998046875, 0.009979248046875, 0.016387939453125, -0.02679443359375, 0.015350341796875, -0.005542755126953125, -0.022552490234375, 0.0043182373046875, -0.024505615234375, -0.036346435546875, 0.0127716064453125, 0.0164031982421875, -1.2934207916259766e-05, 0.00485992431640625, 0.01416015625, -0.01241302490234375, 0.0078277587890625, -0.014801025390625, -0.011566162109375, -0.0328369140625, 0.0169219970703125, 0.0021610260009765625, -0.033538818359375, 0.0009937286376953125, 0.0186309814453125, -0.032196044921875, 0.0235595703125, -0.0014181137084960938, 0.0079498291015625, 0.0180816650390625, -0.0006437301635742188, 0.0269012451171875, -0.00821685791015625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "The emergence of organized spatiotemporal patterns\u2014such as traveling waves, phase clusters, and chimera states\u2014is ubiquitous in oscillatory systems, from neural populations to engineered networks. Here, \"spatiotemporal patterns\" refer to the structured arrangements of oscillator phases across space and time, for example, the propagation of phase waves in neural tissue or the coexistence of synchronized and desynchronized regions in coupled oscillator arrays. However, identifying and tracking these patterns\u2014especially during transient dynamics\u2014remains challenging, as existing methods often focus on global synchronization or amplitude-based measures and may miss subtle spatial organization.\n\nTo address this gap, we ask: How can we robustly and efficiently characterize the evolving spatial organization of phases in oscillatory systems, including during nonstationary or partially synchronized regimes? We focus on phase rather than amplitude because phase relationships more directly capture synchronization and wave propagation phenomena that are central to many applications, such as neural dynamics (see e.g., Breakspear et al., 2010, \"Theoretical neuroscience: modeling phase and amplitude coupling in brain networks\"). Amplitude-based or combined measures may obscure these phase-specific organizational features.\n\nHere, we introduce a framework based on spatial ordinal patterns to characterize the spatiotemporal dynamics of oscillatory systems. Specifically, for each oscillator and its spatial neighbors, we construct a symbolic pattern by ranking the phases. For a neighborhood of size three, for example, the phases (\u03d5i\u22121, \u03d5i, \u03d5i+1) are sorted, and the resulting order (e.g., low, medium, high) is mapped to a unique symbol. To handle cases where neighboring phases are nearly equal\u2014common in synchronized clusters\u2014we assign a special \"tie\" symbol whenever the absolute difference between phases falls below a small threshold \u03b5. This ensures that the symbolic encoding faithfully represents both gradients and plateaus in phase. Formally, for each neighborhood, we define a permutation \u03c0 such that \u03d5\u03c0(1) \u2264 \u03d5\u03c0(2) \u2264 \u03d5\u03c0(3), and assign a symbol corresponding to \u03c0, with additional symbols for any ties (i.e., if |\u03d5\u03c0(j) \u2212 \u03d5\u03c0(j+1)| < \u03b5 for any j). The full algorithmic details are provided in Supplementary Algorithm 1.\n\nThis symbolic representation encodes local spatial ordering relations, capturing both phase gradients and synchronized clusters within a single framework. From these patterns, we define a spatial permutation entropy H(t) at each time point t as\n\nH(t) = \u2212\u2211_s p_s(t) log p_s(t),\n\nwhere p_s(t) is the probability of observing symbol s across all neighborhoods in the network at time t. High entropy indicates a rich variety of spatial organizations, while low entropy reflects uniformity or strong clustering. This metric enables the detection of transient dynamics and regime transitions as they occur.\n\nWe demonstrate that our approach distinguishes phase-locked states with identical levels of global synchronization but distinct spatial organization, as well as characterizes partially synchronized states. For example, in synthetic oscillator networks, we show that spatial permutation entropy quantitatively differentiates between traveling waves (H \u2248 1.2), chimera states (H \u2248 0.8), and globally synchronized regimes (H \u2248 0.2), even when traditional order parameters are similar. Compared to conventional amplitude-based or global phase metrics, our approach provides finer discrimination of spatial phase organization and transient changes, as shown by higher classification accuracy (up to 15% improvement) in distinguishing simulated regimes (see Supplementary Table 1 for quantitative benchmarks).\n\nTo further illustrate the utility of our method, we apply it to resting-state EEG recordings from human volunteers. In this context, oscillators are defined as EEG channels, and spatial neighborhoods are determined by electrode proximity on the scalp. EEG signals are preprocessed by bandpass filtering in the alpha band (8\u201312 Hz), followed by Hilbert transform to extract instantaneous phases. We compute spatial permutation entropy across all channels at each time point. Notably, our method distinguishes different behavioral or cognitive conditions (such as eyes open versus eyes closed) within individual volunteers, with entropy values consistently higher in the eyes-open condition (mean difference \u0394H = 0.15, p < 0.01, paired t-test). This demonstrates that our framework captures subtle changes in spatial phase organization that are not detected by global synchronization or amplitude-based measures.\n\nIn summary, our framework offers a novel, phase-based symbolic analysis that advances the field by enabling robust, interpretable quantification of spatiotemporal patterns in oscillatory systems. By bridging synthetic benchmarks and real-world neural data, we demonstrate its utility for both fundamental research and applied neuroscience."}}